[:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-339661" :question/id #uuid "a793bc80-4687-5211-9dd0-257312689a0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-339661" :question/problem "Which of the following systems are homogeneous?\n\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)$\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "In matrix form, the system\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=A(t)x(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nis *homogeneous* when $f≡0,$ and *inhomogeneous* when $f≢0.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is homogeneous since $f≡0.$\n\n- System II is inhomogeneous since $f=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]≢0.$\n\n- System III is inhomogeneous since $f=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}]≢0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-339661/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28939f65-9fd6-564d-a0c2-73c1038ac898"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-339661"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-339672/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "6d81802a-e676-57f5-b070-eb325bffddb4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339672/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" :answer/id #uuid "9894396e-91e1-5497-af30-a031e9a4f2aa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339672/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "af1044f4-6752-5d17-9dfd-16d4680ed55f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339672/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "0292d3eb-8c30-5b85-a370-59bf5041aaa6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339672/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "ac64420b-5fb2-5241-8ada-65c6e9832b1d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339672/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "cf89ee0d-afe7-575b-a18e-4bb55ce45018" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-339672/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-339672/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-339672/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" "q-339672/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-339672/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-339672/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-339672" :question/id #uuid "0aa1f798-5b62-5ba9-9684-87deffa99c10" :question/math-academy-id "q-339672" :question/problem "Which of the following systems are homogeneous?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)+1\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{1}(t)+6{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)+{e}^{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{2}(t)+3t\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of the form\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $f≡0$ and $g≡0$ (i.e., both $f$ and $g$ are zero functions), then the system is *homogeneous*. Otherwise, if at least one of $f$ or $g$ is not identically zero, the system is *inhomogeneous*.\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is inhomogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≢0$ and $g≢0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=3, &  & b(t)=2, &  & f(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5t) \\\\ c(t)=-1, &  & d(t)=4, &  & g(t)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System II is homogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≡0$ and $g≡0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=-1, &  & b(t)=1, &  & f(t)=0 \\\\ c(t)=5, &  & d(t)=6, &  & g(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System III is inhomogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≢0$ and $g≢0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=4, &  & b(t)=0, &  & f(t)={e}^{2} \\\\ c(t)=0, &  & d(t)=1, &  & g(t)=3t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-339672/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28939f65-9fd6-564d-a0c2-73c1038ac898"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-339672"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-339669/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "a056ae1b-af4e-5bf0-8a7c-26291c697acb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339669/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "1489a6cc-67aa-5fd8-bd86-b2c3a6c24f39" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339669/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "c1c97f00-d2ba-57a7-b057-5ef34a4d1dd3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339669/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "bfe9c011-f8a8-500d-ba31-317f1cbfc04c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339669/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "e6d21818-1e60-5834-9273-8e554900d4b6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339669/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "bb2533cc-915c-5bbd-9f24-9b1df4f32b95" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-339669/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer-field/choices ["q-339669/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-339669/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-339669/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-339669/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-339669/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-339669" :question/id #uuid "64f9647f-5e20-5001-ac5a-8c627a363836" :question/math-academy-id "q-339669" :question/problem "Which of the following systems are homogeneous?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t)+2{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t)+4{e}^{t} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{2}(t)-4{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5){x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of the form\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $f≡0$ and $g≡0$ (i.e., both $f$ and $g$ are zero functions), then the system is *homogeneous*. Otherwise, if at least one of $f$ or $g$ is not identically zero, the system is *inhomogeneous*.\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is inhomogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≢0$ and $g≢0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=-3, &  & b(t)=1, &  & f(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6t) \\\\ c(t)=1, &  & d(t)=-2, &  & g(t)=2{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System II is inhomogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≢0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=1, &  & b(t)=3, &  & f(t)=4{e}^{t} \\\\ c(t)=1, &  & d(t)=-1, &  & g(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System III is homogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≡0$ and $g≡0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=-4, &  & b(t)=1, &  & f(t)=0 \\\\ c(t)=1, &  & d(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5), &  & g(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-339669/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28939f65-9fd6-564d-a0c2-73c1038ac898"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-339669"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-339358/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" :answer/id #uuid "bb653d60-d2dd-55b0-a674-e7745548f369" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339358/selection/71a0fa53da8e2ad79d539774bf13daa79984919151bc8c0231302107306a28ba" :answer/id #uuid "de3a0a1f-a3a6-5d3e-8688-c60a78c3069b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${x}_{3}(t)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339358/selection/966c7488d7134add640357bdedc8b7a4985f081f0e425248157bfa5f446c3f5b" :answer/id #uuid "90b35e4c-4eb7-5be9-886d-df623d4accec" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${x}_{1}(t)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339358/selection/195d0d663d358a9b89f7dccac29f6e88309a977404dab78accb8100eedb05bc5" :answer/id #uuid "18138e69-66ce-5601-a112-de4b10277292" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${x}_{2}(t)$ and ${x}_{3}(t)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339358/selection/ceda492d812324700547a1e4c3428ab46743b8f205f807a806c6141117c706b4" :answer/id #uuid "10451d41-e90a-505b-8295-b2abc9db3312" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${x}_{2}(t)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339358/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "2df613fe-b769-5630-bd66-7a59f9395488" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-339358/selection/71a0fa53da8e2ad79d539774bf13daa79984919151bc8c0231302107306a28ba" :answer-field/choices ["q-339358/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" "q-339358/selection/71a0fa53da8e2ad79d539774bf13daa79984919151bc8c0231302107306a28ba" "q-339358/selection/966c7488d7134add640357bdedc8b7a4985f081f0e425248157bfa5f446c3f5b" "q-339358/selection/195d0d663d358a9b89f7dccac29f6e88309a977404dab78accb8100eedb05bc5" "q-339358/selection/ceda492d812324700547a1e4c3428ab46743b8f205f807a806c6141117c706b4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-339358" :question/id #uuid "c82bd721-0ced-5628-bfd8-e025b1cd1a13" :question/math-academy-id "q-339358" :question/problem "Consider the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t).$ Which of the following vectors are solutions to this equation?\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 5t\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2} \\\\ 2t-8\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we need to substitute each vector into the given matrix differential equation and check if it results in a true statement.\n\nFor ${x}_{1}(t)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(t)^{′} \\\\ (5t)^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 5t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] & \\ne [\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ 5t\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for ${x}_{2}(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{(2{t}^{2})}^{′} \\\\ {(2t-8)}^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2} \\\\ 2t-8\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4t \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & \\ne [\\begin{aligned}4{t}^{2} \\\\ 2t-8\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for ${x}_{3}(t)$, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{({e}^{2t})}^{′} \\\\ {({e}^{t})}^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"${x}_{3}(t)$ only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-339358/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "10420dd6-6f61-5db5-912b-c968d4c649c7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-339358"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-339359/selection/195d0d663d358a9b89f7dccac29f6e88309a977404dab78accb8100eedb05bc5" :answer/id #uuid "c38a973b-7ad2-50cf-ad58-19a6d1d52d41" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${x}_{2}(t)$ and ${x}_{3}(t)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339359/selection/a9a82a60bb82a625d9943d97d775745e299aa7cf482c62b59fdfa28dfc4afcee" :answer/id #uuid "2bf4e45f-a302-583a-bd22-adf4a555d7e4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{3}(t)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339359/selection/966c7488d7134add640357bdedc8b7a4985f081f0e425248157bfa5f446c3f5b" :answer/id #uuid "a7717e34-1526-58af-ac56-b4d430bd6476" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${x}_{1}(t)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339359/selection/c568da3ffba304e60882224d5c53cf3433a8da6b74445c3e811b501438861aab" :answer/id #uuid "addfeb7e-1aec-5fe9-b853-0c3dca698346" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339359/selection/ceda492d812324700547a1e4c3428ab46743b8f205f807a806c6141117c706b4" :answer/id #uuid "983e80e8-6dcf-50a5-9c49-dfb064e16956" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${x}_{2}(t)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339359/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e3f05016-fa57-5df8-a6be-3462d426bec3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-339359/selection/c568da3ffba304e60882224d5c53cf3433a8da6b74445c3e811b501438861aab" :answer-field/choices ["q-339359/selection/195d0d663d358a9b89f7dccac29f6e88309a977404dab78accb8100eedb05bc5" "q-339359/selection/a9a82a60bb82a625d9943d97d775745e299aa7cf482c62b59fdfa28dfc4afcee" "q-339359/selection/966c7488d7134add640357bdedc8b7a4985f081f0e425248157bfa5f446c3f5b" "q-339359/selection/c568da3ffba304e60882224d5c53cf3433a8da6b74445c3e811b501438861aab" "q-339359/selection/ceda492d812324700547a1e4c3428ab46743b8f205f807a806c6141117c706b4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-339359" :question/id #uuid "d06c7ee3-551c-5c09-b315-78ac67d71865" :question/math-academy-id "q-339359" :question/problem "Consider the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t).$ Which of the following vectors are solutions to this equation?\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ t+1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we need to substitute each vector into the given matrix differential equation and check if it results in a true statement.\n\nFor ${x}_{1}(t)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{({e}^{-t})}^{′} \\\\ {(0)}^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for ${x}_{2}(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{(0)}^{′} \\\\ {({e}^{2t})}^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for ${x}_{3}(t)$, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{(t)}^{′} \\\\ {(t+1)}^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ t+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ t+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & \\ne [\\begin{aligned}-t \\\\ 2(t+1)\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-339359/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "10420dd6-6f61-5db5-912b-c968d4c649c7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-339359"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-339744/selection/71a0fa53da8e2ad79d539774bf13daa79984919151bc8c0231302107306a28ba" :answer/id #uuid "48875c46-8f23-53f4-b6ca-8a6b43c83a7f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${x}_{3}(t)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339744/selection/966c7488d7134add640357bdedc8b7a4985f081f0e425248157bfa5f446c3f5b" :answer/id #uuid "bfee026b-fd46-5192-aeb2-a167a7cf44bc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${x}_{1}(t)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339744/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" :answer/id #uuid "f90897d7-bd3f-5a2f-8fc9-c6245e1455aa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339744/selection/a9a82a60bb82a625d9943d97d775745e299aa7cf482c62b59fdfa28dfc4afcee" :answer/id #uuid "030239e1-4bdd-5ffd-93a2-78777e78cefa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{3}(t)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339744/selection/ceda492d812324700547a1e4c3428ab46743b8f205f807a806c6141117c706b4" :answer/id #uuid "9b4e0a2c-27c6-5642-bb91-5c3e266dc689" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${x}_{2}(t)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339744/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "61893fb5-b971-5a7f-b38b-067e13449e21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-339744/selection/966c7488d7134add640357bdedc8b7a4985f081f0e425248157bfa5f446c3f5b" :answer-field/choices ["q-339744/selection/71a0fa53da8e2ad79d539774bf13daa79984919151bc8c0231302107306a28ba" "q-339744/selection/966c7488d7134add640357bdedc8b7a4985f081f0e425248157bfa5f446c3f5b" "q-339744/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" "q-339744/selection/a9a82a60bb82a625d9943d97d775745e299aa7cf482c62b59fdfa28dfc4afcee" "q-339744/selection/ceda492d812324700547a1e4c3428ab46743b8f205f807a806c6141117c706b4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-339744" :question/id #uuid "0a5cb12f-fb18-5374-b444-c1c75a3dde15" :question/math-academy-id "q-339744" :question/problem "Consider the matrix differential equation \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following vectors are solutions to this equation?\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we need to substitute each vector into the given matrix differential equation and check if it results in a true statement.\n\nFor ${x}_{1}(t)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}({e}^{t})^{′} \\\\ ({e}^{2t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for ${x}_{2}(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(0)^{′} \\\\ ({e}^{t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] & \\ne [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}+{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for ${x}_{3}(t)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}({e}^{t})^{′} \\\\ (0)^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & \\ne [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"${x}_{1}(t)$ only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-339744/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "10420dd6-6f61-5db5-912b-c968d4c649c7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-339744"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-339734/selection/ceda492d812324700547a1e4c3428ab46743b8f205f807a806c6141117c706b4" :answer/id #uuid "a3c4201f-ea0c-5013-bd88-4afb6fc05d72" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${x}_{2}(t)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339734/selection/966c7488d7134add640357bdedc8b7a4985f081f0e425248157bfa5f446c3f5b" :answer/id #uuid "be8cb60b-cc94-5d14-9f73-f753f70dd544" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${x}_{1}(t)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339734/selection/a9a82a60bb82a625d9943d97d775745e299aa7cf482c62b59fdfa28dfc4afcee" :answer/id #uuid "88639ab3-dc58-538a-97c0-27572017b354" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{3}(t)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339734/selection/71a0fa53da8e2ad79d539774bf13daa79984919151bc8c0231302107306a28ba" :answer/id #uuid "a68e5542-cbd0-5e88-9137-8095fda676c5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${x}_{3}(t)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339734/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" :answer/id #uuid "d4e4b9a8-1960-52ca-b01a-d16e96e08093" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339734/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "364a373e-e90d-583c-b598-a5ce5197257b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-339734/selection/966c7488d7134add640357bdedc8b7a4985f081f0e425248157bfa5f446c3f5b" :answer-field/choices ["q-339734/selection/ceda492d812324700547a1e4c3428ab46743b8f205f807a806c6141117c706b4" "q-339734/selection/966c7488d7134add640357bdedc8b7a4985f081f0e425248157bfa5f446c3f5b" "q-339734/selection/a9a82a60bb82a625d9943d97d775745e299aa7cf482c62b59fdfa28dfc4afcee" "q-339734/selection/71a0fa53da8e2ad79d539774bf13daa79984919151bc8c0231302107306a28ba" "q-339734/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-339734" :question/id #uuid "17775fc6-5357-537a-a61e-e6f980d1d85c" :question/math-academy-id "q-339734" :question/problem "Consider the matrix differential equation \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following vectors are solutions to this equation?\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{t} \\\\ t{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we need to substitute each vector into the given matrix differential equation and check if it results in a true statement.\n\nFor ${x}_{1}(t)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(t{e}^{t})^{′} \\\\ (t{e}^{t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{t} \\\\ t{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}(t+1){e}^{t} \\\\ (t+1){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{t} \\\\ t{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}(t+1){e}^{t} \\\\ (t+1){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] & \\ne [\\begin{aligned}(t+1){e}^{t} \\\\ (t+1){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for ${x}_{2}(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}({e}^{t})^{′} \\\\ (0)^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & \\ne [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for ${x}_{3}(t)$, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(0)^{′} \\\\ ({e}^{t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] & \\ne [\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"${x}_{1}(t)$ only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-339734/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "10420dd6-6f61-5db5-912b-c968d4c649c7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-339734"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-339353"] :question/problem "Consider the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t).$ Which of the following vectors are solutions to this equation?\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} \\\\ t-4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we need to substitute each vector into the given matrix differential equation and check if it results in a true statement.\n\nFor ${x}_{1}(t)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(0)^{′} \\\\ (5)^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & \\ne [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for ${x}_{2}(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{({e}^{5t})}^{′} \\\\ {({e}^{4t})}^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{5t} \\\\ 4{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{5t} \\\\ 4{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{5t} \\\\ 4{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for ${x}_{3}(t)$, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{({t}^{2})}^{′} \\\\ {(t-4)}^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} \\\\ t-4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} \\\\ t-4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & \\ne [\\begin{aligned}5{t}^{2} \\\\ 4t-16\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"${x}_{2}(t)$ only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21818"] :question/problem "Which of the following systems of differential equations are linear?\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{1}^{2}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}(t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{2}(t))\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of equations that takes the following standard form:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a(t),$ $b(t),$ $c(t),$ $d(t),$ $f(t),$ and $g(t)$ are known functions, and\n\n- $x_1(t)$ and $x_2(t)$ are unknown functions (dependent variables).\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is not a first-order linear system of ODEs since it contains the terms $x_1^2(t)$ and $\\sin(x_2(t)).$\n\n- System II is a first-order linear system of ODEs since it can be written in the standard form, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=8t, &  & b(t)=-1, &  & f(t)=1, \\\\ c(t)=6, &  & d(t)=-2, &  & g(t)={t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only the second system is linear." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21820"] :question/problem "If the following system of differential equations is written in the form $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t),$ then what is the matrix $A?$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)+3{x}_{1}(t)-4{x}_{2}(t)=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)-\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of equations that takes the following standard form:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a(t),$ $b(t),$ $c(t),$ $d(t),$ $f(t),$ and $g(t)$ are known functions, and\n\n- $x_1(t)$ and $x_2(t)$ are unknown functions (dependent variables). \nSuch a system can always be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x'(t) = A\\mathbf x(t) + \\mathbf f(t)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe have the following system.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)+3{x}_{1}(t)-4{x}_{2}(t)=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)-\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting our system in standard form, we get the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can rewrite our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21821"] :question/problem "Which of the following systems are homogeneous?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-2{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=6{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+{t}^{3} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{2}(t)+1\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-2{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of the form\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $f \\equiv 0$ and $g \\equiv 0$ (i.e., both $f$ and $g$ are zero functions), then the system is *homogeneous*. Otherwise, if at least one of $f$ or $g$ is not identically zero, the system is *inhomogeneous*.\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is homogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f \\equiv 0$ and $g \\equiv 0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=-2, &  & b(t)=3, &  & f(t)=0 \\\\ c(t)=6, &  & d(t)=1, &  & g(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System II is inhomogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f \\not\\equiv 0$ and $g \\not\\equiv 0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=1, &  & b(t)=0, &  & f(t)={t}^{3} \\\\ c(t)=0, &  & d(t)=-1, &  & g(t)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System III is homogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f \\equiv 0$ and $g \\equiv 0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=5, &  & b(t)=-1, &  & f(t)=0 \\\\ c(t)=-2, &  & d(t)=4, &  & g(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only the first and third systems are homogeneous." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21819"] :question/problem "Consider the matrix differential equation $[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ Which of the following vectors are solutions to this equation?\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{5t} \\\\ 5{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we need to substitute each vector into the given matrix differential equation and check if it results in a true statement.\n\nFor $\\mathbf{x}_1(t),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{(3{e}^{5t})}^{′} \\\\ {(5{e}^{-3t})}^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{5t} \\\\ 5{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}15{e}^{5t} \\\\ -15{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{5t} \\\\ 5{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}15{e}^{5t} \\\\ -15{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}15{e}^{5t} \\\\ -15{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for $\\mathbf{x}_2(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{(t+5)}^{′} \\\\ {({t}^{2}-3)}^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}t+5 \\\\ {t}^{2}-3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}t+5 \\\\ {t}^{2}-3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}] & \\ne [\\begin{aligned}5t+25 \\\\ -3{t}^{2}+9\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $\\mathbf{x}_3(t),$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{(5{e}^{t})}^{′} \\\\ {(-3{e}^{t})}^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{t} \\\\ -3{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{t} \\\\ -3{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{t} \\\\ -3{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{t} \\\\ -3{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}25{e}^{t} \\\\ 9{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$\\mathbf{x}_1(t)$ only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
